'penarol - am', 'penarol am', 'piaui - pi', 'picos', 'picos - pi', 'placido de castro - ac', 'ponte preta', 'ponte preta - sp', 'ponte preta-sp', 'porto velho - ro', 'porto velho ec', 'portuguesa', 'portuguesa - sp', 'portuguesa desportos', 'portuguesa rj', 'portuguesa-sp', 'potiguar - rn', 'pouso alegre', 'princesa do solimoes', 'princesa do solimoes - am', 'pstc', 'pstc - pr', 'pumas', 'racing club', 'real - rr', 'real ariquemes', 'real atletico', 'real brasilia - df', 'real desportivo - ro', 'real fc', 'real garcilaso', 'real noroeste', 'real noroeste - es', 'real noroeste capixaba - es', 'real noroeste es', 'red bull bragantino', 'red bull bragantino - sp', 'red bull bragantino-sp', 'red bull brasil', 'red bull brasil - sp', 'remo', 'remo - pa', 'remo pa', 'rentistas', 'resende', 'resende - rj', 'resende rj', 'retro', 'retro - pe', 'retro fc brasil', 'rio branco - ac', 'rio branco - es', 'rio branco - vn - es', 'rio branco ac', 'rio branco es', 'river', 'river - pi', 'river ac', 'river plate', 'river plate - se', 'river plate-uru', 'rondoniense - ro', 'rondonopolis - mt', 'rosario central', 's.francisco - pa', 'salgueiro', 'salgueiro - pe', 'sampaio correa', 'sampaio correa - ma', 'san jose', 'san lorenzo', 'santa cruz', 'santa cruz - pe', 'santa cruz - rn', 'santa cruz - rs', 'santa cruz fc', 'santa cruz rn', 'santa cruz rs', 'santa cruz-pe', 'santa quiteria futebol clube - ma', 'santa rita', 'santa rita - al', 'santo andre', 'santo andre - sp', 'santo andre sp', 'santos', 'santos - ap', 'santos - sp', 'santos ap', 'santos laguna', 'santos-sp', 'sao bento', 'sao bento - sp', 'sao bernardo', 'sao bernardo - sp', 'sao caetano', 'sao caetano - sp', 'sao domingos futebol clube - se', 'sao francisco ac', 'sao francisco pa', 'sao jose - poa', 'sao jose - rs', 'sao jose pa', 'sao jose rs', 'sao luiz', 'sao luiz - rs', 'sao mateus es - es', 'sao paulo', 'sao paulo - sp', 'sao paulo-sp', 'sao raimundo - pa', 'sao raimundo - rr', 'sao raimundo am', 'sao raimundo pa', 'sao raimundo rr', 'sc genus', 'se gama', 'ser caxias - rs', 'sergipe', 'sergipe - se', 'serra', 'serra f. c. - es', 'sete de setembro', 'sinop - mt', 'sinop fc', 'sobradinho', 'sobradinho - df', 'sousa ec', 'souza - pb', 'sport', 'sport - pe', 'sport boys', 'sport recife', 'sport-pe', 'sporting cristal', 'suzano sp', 'talleres', 'the strongest', 'tigre', 'tigres', 'tijuana', 'tocantinopolis', 'tocantinopolis - to', 'tocantinopolis ec', 'toledo - pr', 'toledo ec', 'tolima', 'toluca', 'tombense', 'tombense - mg', 'tombense mg', 'trem - ap', 'trem ap', 'treze', 'treze - pb', 'trujillanos', 'tubarao', 'tubarao - sc', 'tuna luso', 'tuntum ec', 'tupi', 'tupi - mg', 'tupi mg', 'uberlandia', 'uberlandia - mg', 'uniao - mt', 'uniao de rondonopolis - mt', 'uniao rondonopolis', 'uniclinic - ce', 'uniclinic ce', 'union espanola', 'union la calera', 'universidad catolica', 'universidad de chile', 'universidad de concepcion', 'universitario', 'universitario de sucre', 'universitario-per', 'urt', 'urt - mg', 'urt mg', 'vasco', 'vasco da gama - rj', 'vasco da gama rj', 'vasco da gama-rj', 'velez sarsfield', 'veranopolis - rs', 'vila nova', 'vila nova - go', 'vilhena - ro', 'vilhena ro', 'vilhenense - ro', 'vilhenense ec', 'villa nova', 'villa nova - mg', 'vitoria', 'vitoria - ba', 'vitoria da conquista', 'vitoria da conquista - ba', 'vitoria ec', 'vitoria es', 'vitoria f. c. - es', 'vitoria-ba', 'volta redonda', 'volta redonda - rj', 'votuporanguense - sp', 'xv de piracicaba - sp', 'xv piracicaba', 'ypiranga', 'ypiranga - ap', 'ypiranga - rs', 'ypiranga ap', 'ypiranga rs', 'zamora', 'zulia']\n","exit":0,"description":"Extract all unique team names and group by normalized key","truncated":false},"title":"Extract all unique team names and group by normalized key","time":{"start":1782086797134,"end":1782086797159}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK, so FIFA data has some Brazilian clubs (Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Botafogo) but the dataset appears to be a somewhat modified/fictional player names version (e.g., \"L. Messi\" is there but many Brazilian club players have invented-looking names like \"Juiano Mestres\"). It's a benchmark dataset, fine.\n\nNow I have enough understanding. Let me design the implementation.\n\nArchitecture:\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py`\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py` - normalize_team_name, parse_date, canonical team keys\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py` - load all CSVs into unified MatchRecord dataclass list and Player list, with team normalization\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/query_engine.py` - QueryEngine class with methods for all 5 categories\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/mcp_server.py` - FastMCP server exposing tools that wrap QueryEngine\n- `brazilian_soccer_mcp/models.py` - dataclasses for Match, Player, TeamStats\n- `pyproject.toml`\n- `tests/conftest.py`\n- `tests/test_*.py` - BDD-style tests with Gherkin docstrings\n- Update `README.md`\n\nLet me think about team normalization. The datasets use:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" (with state suffix)\n- \"Palmeiras\" (no suffix)\n- \"Athletico Paranaense\" / \"Athletico-PR\" / \"Athletico-PR\" / \"Atlético-MG\"\n- \"Corinthians\" (misspelled) vs \"Corinthians\" - both appear (note the CSVs use \"Corinthians\" not \"Corinthians\"! It's a common misspelling in the dataset)\n- \"Sao Paulo\" / \"São Paulo\"\n- \"Gremio\" / \"Grêmio\"\n- \"Botafogo RJ\" / \"Botafogo-RJ\" / \"Botafogo\"\n- \"America MG\" / \"América - MG\" / \"América-MG\"\n- \"Sport-PE\" / \"Sport\"\n\nI'll create a normalizer that:\n1. Strips state suffixes (anything after \"-\" or \" - \" or in parentheses, etc.)\n2. Removes accents (unicodedata normalize NFKD + strip accents) OR keep accents but compare normalized\n3. Lowercases\n4. Maps known aliases to canonical names\n\nActually, simplest robust approach: create a `canonical_key(name)` that:\n- Strips state suffix patterns: ` -XX`, `-XX` (two-letter state), `(antigo...)`, etc.\n- Removes accents\n- Lowercases\n- Strips whitespace\n- Maps a few known synonym keys (e.g., \"corinthians\" -> \"corinthians\" canonical; \"corinthians\" stays; but \"corinthians\" vs \"corinthians\" - they're the same spelling in data; the spec mentions \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" full name)\n\nWait the spec says full names like \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" might appear. But in our actual data, do they? Let me not worry too much; I'll build a reasonable normalizer.\n\nFor the canonical key, I'll generate a key like \"corinthians\", \"palmeiras\", \"saopaulo\", \"gremio\", \"flamengo\", \"fluminense\", \"vasco\", \"botafogo\", \"santos\", \"cruzeiro\", \"atletico\", \"athletico\", \"internacional\", \"gremio\", \"coritiba\", \"bahia\", \"sport\", \"nautico\", \"figueirense\", \"ponte preta\", \"portuguesa\", etc.\n\nThe \"Athletico Paranaense\" vs \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-MG\" — these are different teams! \"Athletico Paranaense\" is Athletico-PR (Paraná), \"Atlético-MG\" is Atlético Mineiro. So stripping state alone and keying by name \"athletico\"/\"atletico\" would conflate them if both reduce to same. Actually \"Athletico\" and \"Atlético\" are different strings even after accent removal: \"athletico\" vs \"atletico\" (h vs no h). They stay distinct. Good. But \"Athletico Paranaense\" reduces to \"athletico paranaense\" and \"Athletico-PR\" reduces to \"athletico\" — these would NOT match! That's a problem.\n\nHmm. So I need a smarter approach. Maybe map known aliases. Let me build an alias map for common Brazilian teams:\n\nCanonical -> list of alias keys:\n- \"corinthians\" -> [\"corinthians\", \"corinthians-XX\", \"sport club corinthians paulista\"]\n- \"palmeiras\" -> [\"palmeiras\"]\n- \"sao paulo\" -> [\"sao paulo\", \"saopaulo\"]\n- \"santos\" -> [\"santos\"]\n- \"flamengo\" -> [\"flamengo\"]\n- \"fluminense\" -> [\"fluminense\"]\n- \"vasco\" -> [\"vasco da gama\", \"vasco\"]\n- \"botafogo\" -> [\"botafogo\"]\n- \"gremio\" -> [\"gremio\"]\n- \"cruzeiro\" -> [\"cruzeiro\"]\n- \"atletico-mg\" -> [\"atletico-mg\", \"atletico mineiro\", \"atletico mg\", \"america mg\"? no]\n- \"athletico-pr\" -> [\"athletico-pr\", \"athletico paranaense\", \"athletico pr\"]\n- \"internacional\" -> [\"internacional\", \"inter\", \"internacional-rs\"]\n- \"coritiba\" -> [\"coritiba\"]\n- \"bahia\" -> [\"bahia\", \"ec bahia\", \"bahia de feira\"? no that's a different club]\n- \"sport\" -> [\"sport\", \"sport-PE\", \"sport recife\"]\n- \"nautico\" -> [\"nautico\"]\n- \"figueirense\" -> [\"figueirense\"]\n- \"ponte preta\" -> [\"ponte pretas\"? no, \"ponte preta\"]\n- \"portuguesa\" -> [\"portuguesa\", \"portuguesa-sP\"]\n- \"goias\" -> [\"goias\", \"goiás\"]\n- \"paysandu\" -> [\"paysandu\"]\n- \"guarani\" -> [\"guarani\"]\n- \"vitoria\" -> [\"vitoria\", \"vitória\"]\n- \"fortaleza\" -> [\"fortaleza\"]\n- \"juventude\" -> [\"juventude\"]\n- \"criciuma\" -> [\"criciuma\", \"criciúma\"]\n- \"avai\" -> [\"avai\", \"avaí\"]\n- \"chapecoense\" -> [\"chapecoense\"]\n- \"america-mg\" -> [\"america-mg\", \"america mg\", \"américa - mg\", \"américa-mg\"]\n- \"america-rj\" -> [...] - distinct\n- \"atletico-go\" -> [\"atletico-go\", \"atletico-goianiense\"]\n- \"cuiaba\" -> [\"cuiaba\"]\n- \"-operario\"? etc.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll build a function that produces a canonical key by:\n1. Removing accents\n2. Lowercasing\n3. Removing content in parentheses\n4. Removing state suffixes: patterns like ` -XX`, `-XX` where XX is a 2-letter state code, or known state names\n5. Removing punctuation\n6. Stripping whitespace\n7. Then applying an alias map for the remaining few special cases\n\nThen for Athletico Paranaense vs Athletico-PR, both would reduce to \"athletico\" (after removing \"-PR\" and \" paranaense\"? no, \"paranaense\" wouldn't be removed). Hmm.\n\nActually the BR-Football-Dataset has \"Athletico Paranaense\" (full), while Brasileirao has \"Athletico-PR\" (wait, no — Brasileirao home sample showed \"Atletico-MG\", \"Atletico-GO\" but not Athletico-PR explicitly; the hist file showed \"Athletico-PR\"). And BR-Football has \"Athletico Paranaense\".\n\nSo I want \"Athletico Paranaense\" and \"Athletico-PR\" to map to the same canonical. Approach: build a comprehensive alias dict mapping many variant forms to a canonical ID. I'll write a function `canonical_team_id(name)` that:\n1. Normalize: strip parens, strip accents, lowercase, remove punctuation, collapse whitespace\n2. Remove trailing state code patterns: \"-XX\" or \" XX\" where XX in known state list {SP,RJ,MG,RS,PR,SC,PE,BA,GO,CE,DF,ES,MS,MT,PA,PB,PI,RN,TO,SE,AL,AM,AP,RR,RO,AC,MA}\n3. Also remove standalone state names at end: \"paranaense\"→ remove? Better: just strip \" paranaense\" if starts with \"athletico\". Hmm too specific.\n\nAlternative simpler approach: Use a curated alias dictionary `TEAM_ALIASES` mapping many specific known forms. For anything not in the dict, fall back to the generic normalizer. This handles the well-known Brazilian teams. Let me build it.\n\nLet me design `TEAM_ALIASES: dict[canonical_id, list[alias_keys]]` and an inverted lookup built at import time. The canonical_id could be a friendly name like \"Corinthians-SP\" or just \"Corinthians\". I'll use a canonical name (without state) like \"Corinthians\", \"Palmeiras\", \"Sao Paulo\", \"Santos\", \"Flamengo\", \"Fluminense\", \"Vasco da Gama\", \"Botafogo\", \"Gremio\", \"Cruzeiro\", \"Atletico-MG\", \"Athletico-PR\", \"Internacional\", \"Coritiba\", \"Bahia\", \"Sport\", \"Nautico\", \"Figueirense\", \"Ponte Preta\", \"Portuguesa\", \"Goias\", \"Paysandu\", \"Guarani\", \"Vitoria\", \"Fortaleza\", \"Juventude\", \"Criciuma\", \"Avai\", \"Chapecoense\", \"America-MG\", \"Atletico-GO\", \"Cuiaba\", \"Operario-PR\", \"Confianca\", etc.\n\nBut I should focus on teams that actually appear in the data. Let me extract all unique home/away team names across all 5 match files, then build a normalizer that groups them. Let me do that programmatically and inspect the