rasileirao (2003-2019)\" — or just merge. Let me label matches from the historical file as competition=\"Brasileirao\" with season from Ano. And Brasileirao_Matches.csv as \"Brasileirao\". The BR-Football-Dataset has tournament column (\"Copa do Brasil\", \"Serie A\", \"Serie B\", \"Serie C\"). I'll map \"Serie A\" → \"Brasileirao Serie A\" or just keep \"Serie A\". Let me keep the tournament string as-is but also add a normalized competition category. Hmm, to keep simple: store `competition` as the raw label from the source, and add `competition_group` as a normalized enum: BRASILEIRAO, COPA_DO_BRASIL, LIBERTADORES, SERIE_B, SERIE_C, etc. Actually the spec's competition filter examples: \"Brasileirão, Copa do Brasil, Libertadores\". So I'll provide a normalized competition name:\n  - Brasileirao_Matches.csv → \"Brasileirao\"\n  - novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → \"Brasileirao\" (historical; same competition, merge)\n  - Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → \"Copa do Brasil\"\n  - Libertadores_Matches.csv → \"Libertadores\"\n  - BR-Football-Dataset.csv tournament \"Copa do Brasil\" → \"Copa do Brasil\"; \"Serie A\" → \"Brasileirao\"; \"Serie B\" → \"Serie B\"; \"Serie C\" → \"Serie C\"\n- round: Optional[int]\n- stage: Optional[str] (for Libertadores)\n- stadium: Optional[str]\n- source_file: str\n- home_state, away_state: Optional[str]\n- extra stats (corners, shots, attacks) when available from BR-Football file\n\nI'll load all into a single list `matches` of Match dataclass. For BR-Football, it has additional stats; I'll include them as optional fields on Match (corners, shots, attacks, halftime scores). To avoid bloat, I'll put extras in a dict.\n\nActually, simpler: I'll keep each source's DataFrame accessible, plus a unified long DataFrame `matches_df` with columns: date, home_id, home_name, away_id, away_name, home_goal, away_goal, season, competition, competition_raw, round, stage, stadium, home_state, away_state, source. Plus a separate `stats_df` for the BR-Football extras (joined by date+home+away or by index). Hmm. For benchmark, I'll mostly use the unified matches_df. Extras optional.\n\nLet me keep a unified pandas DataFrame approach. Pandas is fast enough (35k rows total). The query engine will operate on this DataFrame.\n\nPlayers: load fifa_data.csv into `players_df` with selected columns: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight, Preferred Foot, and a few skill ratings (Crossing, Finishing, Dribbling, etc.). For queries: search by name (substring, accent-insensitive), filter by nationality, filter by club (with team normalization so \"Flamengo\" matches Flamengo-RJ? but FIFA clubs are different naming — actually FIFA clubs for Brazilian teams use names like \"Cruzeiro\", \"Fluminense\", \"Santos\", \"Botafogo\" without state. So club normalization: I'll apply the same team_key normalizer to clubs. So querying \"Flamengo\" via FIFA returns players whose club canonical_id == \"flamengo\" — but earlier we saw FIFA has no Flamengo club! The FIFA dataset has Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Botafogo, etc. So \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" → 0 players from FIFA. That's the truth. Fine.\n\nNow the queries. Let me design the QueryEngine API methods (these become MCP tools):\n- `search_matches(team=None, vs_team=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=None)` → list of match dicts\n- `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` → record (wins_a, wins_b, draws, goals, list of matches)\n- `team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None, home_away=None)` → wins/draws/losses/goals_for/goals_against/win_rate\n- `best_team_record(competition=None, season=None, home_away=None)` → ranking\n- `top_scorers_by_team`? — Not inferable from match data (no scorer names). Skip or note. The spec says \"Top scorers (if inferable from data)\". We can compute \"teams that scored most goals\" but not individual player scorers. I'll provide `most_goals_scored(competition, season)` returning team ranking by goals.\n- `search_player(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, limit=None, sort_by='Overall')` → list of player dicts\n- `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)` → sorted by Overall\n- `players_at_club(club)` → list + avg rating\n- `competition_standings(competition, season)` → computed standings (points, W/D/L, goals)\n- `competition_champion(competition, season)` → top team\n- `relegated_teams(competition, season, n=4)` → bottom n teams\n- `average_goals_per_match(competition=None, season=None)` → float\n- `home_vs_away_performance(competition=None, season=None)` → home win rate, away win rate, draw rate\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` → matches sorted by goal diff\n- `derbies_in_season(season, competition=None)` → matches between known rival pairs (curated list)\n\nDerbies: curated rival pairs (canonical IDs): fla-flu (flamengo vs fluminense), gre-nal (gremio vs internacional), san-pal (santos vs palmeiras), cor-sao (corinthians vs sao paulo), vas-bot (vasco vs botafogo), atletico-mg vs cruzeiro (Clube do Mai), etc. I'll include a handful.\n\nFor competition_standings: compute from matches in that competition+season. For a double round-robin league (Brasileirao), points = 3*W + 1*D, W/D/L computed from team's perspective across all its matches (home+away). Goals_for = sum goals scored, goals_against = sum conceded. Sort by points desc, then wins desc, then goal difference desc. Return top N.\n\nFor \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → champion = standings[0]. For 2019, the data: Brasileirao_Matches.csv covers 2012-2023ish. Let me check the seasons range.\n\nLet me also be careful: the historical 2003-2019 file and the Brasileirao_Matches.csv might overlap for 2012-2019? The historical file is 2003-2019 (6886 matches ≈ 17 seasons × ~400). Brasileirao_Matches is 2012-2023 (4180 ≈ 12 seasons × ~380). There's potential overlap 2012-2019! That would double-count. Let me check. Actually the spec lists them as separate datasets to use; the benchmark probably doesn't care about dedup. But for standings, double-counting would break. Let me check overlap.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eeca7eda0001DlSHcyy1B0cYjz","sessionID":"ses_1135b46adffeahTNKaUmDz4HW0","messageID":"msg_eeca68826001VulmAxgIT05ViA"}}
