{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782086921982,"sessionID":"ses_1135b46adffeahTNKaUmDz4HW0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_9f5c6f796ff44e22a5c25f1d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"import pytest_bdd; print('pytest_bdd', pytest_bdd.__version__)\" 2>&1 | tail -2; pip install pytest-bdd 2>&1 | tail -4","description":"Check pytest-bdd availability and install"},"output":"  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\nAttributeError: module 'pytest_bdd' has no attribute '__version__'\nRequirement already satisfied: iniconfig in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from pytest>=7.0.0->pytest-bdd) (2.3.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: pluggy<2.0,>=1.5 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from pytest>=7.0.0->pytest-bdd) (1.6.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: MarkupSafe>=0.9.2 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from Mako->pytest-bdd) (3.0.3)\nRequirement already satisfied: six>=1.15 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from parse-type->pytest-bdd) (1.17.0)\n","metadata":{"output":"  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\nAttributeError: module 'pytest_bdd' has no attribute '__version__'\nRequirement already satisfied: iniconfig in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from pytest>=7.0.0->pytest-bdd) (2.3.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: pluggy<2.0,>=1.5 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from pytest>=7.0.0->pytest-bdd) (1.6.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: MarkupSafe>=0.9.2 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from Mako->pytest-bdd) (3.0.3)\nRequirement already satisfied: six>=1.15 in /Users/maui/dve/experiments/retort/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from parse-type->pytest-bdd) (1.17.0)\n","exit":0,"description":"Check pytest-bdd availability and install","truncated":false},"title":"Check pytest-bdd availability and install","time":{"start":1782086921978,"end":1782086921980}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's overlap between Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) and the historical file (2003-2019), AND the BR-Football Serie A (2014-2023). So there's triple overlap for some seasons. For example, 2019 Brasileirao: 380 in Brasileirao_Matches, 380 in hist, 380 in BR-Football Serie A = 3x the same matches.\n\nThis is real overlap. For aggregation queries (standings, average goals), I need to dedupe to avoid triple-counting. I'll dedupe by (date, home_team_canonical, away_team_canonical) — but the same match may have slightly different dates between sources (kickoff time differences) and team name differences. Dedup is tricky.\n\nHmm. For the benchmark, maybe the cleanest approach: keep each source separate but for aggregation queries that need a single canonical set of match results, use a \"preferred\" source per competition+season to avoid double counting. Or dedupe by (date, home_id, away_id) keeping first occurrence.\n\nLet me think about what the tests will check. The TASK.md's success criteria: \"Can search and return match data from all provided CSV files\" (so search across all), \"Can calculate basic statistics (wins, losses, goals)\" (per team across all? or per season?). The example \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" expects a single champion (Flamengo with 90 pts). If I triple-count, the points would be ~270 instead of 90. So I MUST dedupe for standings.\n\nStrategy: I'll build a unified matches table, then provide a `drop_duplicates` for aggregation. Dedup key: (season, home_id, away_id, date) — but date may differ. Actually for the same match, the date is the same day (kickoff time may differ but the day is same). Let me use (season, home_id, away_id, date_day). If home/away swapped between sources? No, home is consistent. Also the home_id and away_id canonical must match across sources (e.g., \"Palmeiras\" vs \"Palmeiras-SP\" → both \"palmeiras\"). So dedup key (season, home_id, away_id, date) should work for same-day same-team matches. But what if the same two teams played twice in a season (home and away legs)? Those would have different dates, so distinct keys — good. What about the same match appearing in two sources with the same date but one source has the home/away flipped? Unlikely.\n\nAlso, the historical file uses DD/MM/YYYY; Brasileirao uses YYYY-MM-DD HH:SS. After parsing to a date (day), they'd match. Let me parse dates to a date object (day only, ignore time).\n\nLet me verify dedup: for 2019 Flamengo champion, compute standings from deduped Brasileirao 2019 matches. Let me sanity-check after building. The expected champion 2019 is Flamengo (90 pts). Let me confirm by computing.\n\nLet me proceed to write code now. I'll dedupe by (season, competition_group, home_id, away_id, date). For BR-Football \"Serie A\" → competition_group \"Brasileirao\", merged with Brasileirao_Matches. For Copa do Brasil: BR-Football has 1123 + Brazilian_Cup_Matches 1337 (2012-2023ish). Overlap likely for 2014-2023. Dedupe similarly.\n\nFor Libertadores: only Libertadores_Matches.csv. No overlap with BR-Football (which is only Brazilian domestic). Good.\n\nFor Serie B, Serie C: only BR-Football. Good.\n\nI'll mark competition_group:\n- \"Brasileirao\" — from Brasileirao_Matches, hist (2003-2019), BR-Football Serie A\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" — Brazilian_Cup_Matches + BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- \"Libertadores\" — Libertadores_Matches\n- \"Serie B\" — BR-Football \"Serie B\"\n- \"Serie C\" — BR-Football \"Serie C\"\n\nAnd competition (display) similar.\n\nDedup approach: build a unified df with a `dup_key` = (season, competition_group, home_id, away_id, date). Use drop_duplicates keeping first, with a source priority (prefer BR-Football for stats? or prefer Brasileirao_Matches). For standings we just need W/D/L and goals; any source works. I'll keep first by source order: Brasileirao_Matches, then hist, then BR-Football Serie A, then Cup, then BR-Football Copa do Brasil, then Libertadores. Actually for matches that have extended stats (corners/shots) only in BR-Football, I'd want to keep that source for stat queries. But for standings, the basic result is identical. Hmm.\n\nBetter: keep ALL rows but add a dedup helper. For aggregation queries that count results, use a deduped view (`matches_unique`). For match listing queries (search_matches), return all (with source label) — the user can see the match appears in multiple datasets. Actually that might be confusing (3 duplicate entries per match). But the spec says \"Can search and return match data from all provided CSV files\" — so showing sources is OK. Hmm. For head-to-head \"Flamengo 12 wins\" — if triple-counted, wins would be ~36. The example shows \"12 wins\" — so head-to-head must dedupe.\n\nSo for all aggregate/stat queries, use deduped. For search_matches listing, I'll dedupe too but include source info (list which sources contain it). Actually the example \"Flamengo vs Fluminense ... (15 more matches in dataset)\" and \"Head-to-head: Flamengo 12 wins\" implies a deduped count. Let me dedupe for everything by default, and offer a `include_all_sources` flag for raw search if needed. Default dedupe.\n\nOK. Let me also keep the extended BR-Football stats accessible via a separate stats table keyed by (date, home_id, away_id) for the matches that have them, for the \"biggest wins\" etc. Actually \"biggest wins\" just needs score; deduped matches suffice.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nPlan of files:\n1. `pyproject.toml` — package metadata, deps: pandas, mcp. Test deps: pytest. I'll use a simple setup with `pip install -e .` model. Actually for tests to run, I need the package importable. I'll create pyproject with `[project]` and a `tests` target. Use `setuptools` or just a simple package layout with `pip install -e .`. I'll add a `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` with testpaths.\n\n2. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py` — exports QueryEngine, load functions.\n\n3. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py` — `canonical_team_id`, `team_display_name`, `parse_date`, `club_key` (alias of canonical_team_id), STATE set, ALIASES dict, DERBIES list.\n\n4. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/models.py` — dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamStats, StandingRow, HeadToHead. (Optional; I might just return dicts. But dataclasses make tests cleaner. I'll use dataclasses with as_dict.)\n\n5. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py` — `load_all(data_dir)` → (matches_df, players_df, stats_df). Returns a `DataBundle` namedtuple. Plus a `build_query_engine`.\n\n6. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/query_engine.py` — `QueryEngine` class holding the DataBundle, with methods. Each method returns structured data (dicts or dataclass instances).\n\n7. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/mcp_server.py` — `build_server(engine)` returning a FastMCP instance with registered tools wrapping engine methods. Plus `main()` entrypoint.\n\n8. `tests/conftest.py` — fixture `engine` that loads data once (session scope). Tests use it.\n\n9. `tests/test_match_queries.py`, `test_team_queries.py`, `test_player_queries.py`, `test_competition_queries.py`, `test_statistical_analysis.py`, `test_normalization.py` — each with BDD-style Gherkin docstring at top of test functions (Given/When/Then as comments or via pytest-bdd? The spec shows Gherkin format. I'll use plain pytest with Gherkin in docstrings as the \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" approach, since pytest-bdd may not be installed). Let me check pytest-bdd.\n\nActually the prompts.txt mentions \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\" — but that's a sample prompt, not my instruction. My instruction is just \"implement everything TASK.md asks\" + \"tests pass\". The TASK.md testing approach shows Gherkin BDD scenarios. I'll implement tests with Gherkin-style docstrings and Given/When/Then arranged as test steps using plain asserts. I won't require pytest-bdd