  2019-11-10          bahia\n2989  2019-11-13          vasco\n3004  2019-11-27       ceara ce\n3028  2019-12-05           avai\n","exit":0,"description":"Compare Flamengo home dates brfootball vs brasileirao","truncated":false},"title":"Compare Flamengo home dates brfootball vs brasileirao","time":{"start":1782087346927,"end":1782087346973}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So for Flamengo vs Palmeiras 2019, all three sources have the same dates (2019-09-01 and 2019-12-01). Dedup by (competition, season, home_id, away_id, date) should collapse these 2 fixtures into 2 unique rows. And indeed, the dedup seems to have worked for THIS match (sources include all three). \n\nBut total unique Flamengo 2019 Brasileirao = 49. With 114 raw. 114/49 ≈ 2.3x. So dedup partially worked but not fully. The sources tuples show many single-source matches: ('brfootball',): 7, ('brasileirao',): 4, ('hist',): 2, ('brfootball','hist'):2, ('brasileiro','hist'):5. So 7+4+2+2+5=20 matches are NOT deduped across sources even though they should be (same fixture appearing in brasileirao AND brfootball). \n\nWhy? Because the dates differ between sources for those fixtures, OR the home/away ids differ. Wait, the sources tuples like ('brfootball',) mean that match exists ONLY in brfootball among the unique set — meaning the brasileirao version of the same fixture was deduped away OR had a different date. Actually if both sources had the same date+teams, they'd merge into one row with sources ('brasileirao','brfootball'). A row with sources ('brfootball',) means no brasileirao/hist row shared that exact (date, home, away) key — so the brasileirao version had a different date for that fixture.\n\nLet me verify: pick a Flamengo 2019 match that's brfootball-only in unique, and find its brasileirao counterpart.\n\nActually, simpler fix: dedup WITHOUT date in the key. For league (Brasileirao, Serie B, Serie C) round-robin, the same pair (home, away) in the same season is a unique fixture (they play home-and-away, but those are different (home,away) orderings, so still unique per ordered pair). For Copa do Brasil (knockout, two legs), the same ordered pair could meet twice? Rare. For Libertadores, group stage: same ordered pair meets once; knockout two legs = two different home/away orderings. So dedup by (competition, season, home_id, away_id) WITHOUT date should work for most, but Copa do Brasil knockout has two legs where the SAME ordered home/away might recur? Actually two legs are home then away (different orderings), so ordered pair is unique per leg. But a team could host twice in a tie if... no. \n\nHowever, dropping date entirely could merge two genuinely different matches that happen to share ordered pair in a season (e.g., a league match + a cup match — but those are different competition). Within same competition+season+ordered pair, generally unique. But there's risk: Brasileirao is double round robin — Flamengo hosts Palmeiras once, Palmeiras hosts Flamengo once. Different ordered pairs. Good. So (competition, season, home_id, away_id) is a safe dedup key for league. For knockout cups, a tie is two legs (A home then B home) — different ordered pairs. So safe.\n\nBut wait — what about the same two teams meeting in group stage AND knockout of Libertadores in same season? E.g., two teams in same group then meet in knockout. Group stage: ordered pair once each direction. Knockout: two legs (different directions). Could a team host the same opponent twice in one Libertadores season (once in group, once in knockout leg)? Yes! E.g., group: A hosts B. Knockout first leg: A hosts B again. Same (season, home=A, away=B). Dropping date would merge them. That's bad.\n\nHmm. So dropping date entirely is risky for Libertadores. But keeping date fails to dedup across sources when dates differ.\n\nBetter approach: dedup by (competition, season, home_id, away_id, round) for league (round disambiguates the two legs since they're in different rounds), and for cups keep date. But brfootball has no round. Hmm.\n\nAlternative robust approach: dedup by (competition, season, home_id, away_id, date) but NORMALIZE dates by attempting to match across sources. Actually the real problem is some fixtures have different dates between brasileirao and brfootball. Let me check WHY — maybe brfootball uses the actual played date and brasileirao uses scheduled date, or one is off by a day.\n\nLet me inspect a brfootball-only Flamengo 2019 fixture and find the brasileirao version.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecaee580001BeLSKZPkni8sMu","sessionID":"ses_1135b46adffeahTNKaUmDz4HW0","messageID":"msg_eecae79a7001tMksIcVfs3y3mY"}}
