6fe959f7/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py","patch":"Index: /private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-1zdmpvud/retort-7c2f6fe959f7/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py\n===================================================================\n--- /private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-1zdmpvud/retort-7c2f6fe959f7/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py\n+++ /private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-1zdmpvud/retort-7c2f6fe959f7/brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py\n@@ -59,9 +59,9 @@\n ]),\n (\"athletico_pr\", [\n     \"athletico pr\", \"athletico paranaense\", \"athletico paranaense pr\",\n     \"atletico pr\", \"atletico paranaense\", \"atletico paranaense pr\",\n-    \"ca parana\", \"parana\",\n+    \"ca parana\",\n ]),\n (\"atletico_go\", [\"atletico go\", \"atletico goianiense\", \"atletico goianiense go\"]),\n (\"coritiba\", [\"coritiba\", \"coritiba pr\"]),\n (\"bahia\", [\"bahia\", \"bahia ba\", \"ec bahia\", \"esporte clube bahia\"]),\n","additions":1,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalizers.py","time":{"start":1782087485165,"end":1782087485168}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Many minor clubs have unstripped state suffixes. For Serie A standings, the key clubs that appear with state suffix and need stripping to match brfootball's no-suffix form: csa, ceara, santa cruz (PE), parana, joinville, bragantino (PA - different club), santo andre, etc.\n\nThe critical ones for the 2019 Brasileirao standings are the 20 Serie A 2019 clubs. Let me identify them and ensure consistent canonicalization. The 2019 Serie A clubs (from brasileirao source): Flamengo, Santos, Palmeiras, Gremio, Sao Paulo, Atletico-MG, Athletico-PR, Internacional, Corinthians, Botafogo, Fluminense, Bahia, Vasco, Ceara, Fortaleza, Cruzeiro, CSA, Chapecoense, Avai, Goias.\n\nOf these, the ones with mismatched ids vs brfootball:\n- Ceara: brasileirao \"Ceará-CE\" → \"ceara ce\"; brfootball \"Ceara\" → \"ceara\". MISMATCH. Need \"ceara ce\" → \"ceara\" and \"ceara\" → \"ceara\".\n- CSA: brasileirao \"CSA-AL\" → \"csa al\"; brfootball \"CSA\" → \"csa\". MISMATCH. Need \"csa al\" → \"csa\", \"csa\" → \"csa\".\n- Others (Fortaleza, Bahia, Chapecoense, Avai, Goias, etc.) already aliased.\n\nAlso need to make sure brfootball spellings for Athletico-PR etc. map right. brfootball uses \"Athletico Paranaense\" → \"athletico_pr\" (aliased). Good. \"Atletico-MG\" in brasileirao → \"atletico_mg\" (aliased). brfootball \"America Mineiro\"? Let me check — brfootball uses \"America MG\" → \"america_mg\" (aliased). Good.\n\nSo I need to add aliases for: csa, ceara, santa_cruz (PE — distinct from RN/RS Santa Cruz), parana_clube, joinville, santo_andre, etc. — at least the Serie A ones. Let me add a broader set covering all clubs that appear in Serie A/B across the dataset, to be safe for standings queries on those competitions too.\n\nActually, the cleanest general fix: change the fallback to strip a trailing state token, BUT keep a \"no-strip\" blacklist of base names where state is semantically required. The blacklist = bases where two different states produce legitimately different major clubs that we DON'T want merged: {atletico, athletico, america, nacional, botafogo, portuguesa, santa cruz, sao jose, sao raimundo, sao francisco, river, rio branco, operario, ypiranga, vitoria, bahia de feira, penarol, trem, galvez, inter, nacional, bragantino, sao paulo? no}. \n\nHmm, but \"botafogo\" we DO want botafogo-rj and bare botafogo merged (major club), but NOT botafogo-sp / botafogo-pb. So stripping state from \"botafogo rj\" → \"botafogo\" (good, matches bare), but \"botafogo sp\" → \"botafogo\" (bad, merges with Botafogo-RJ). So botafogo is in the blacklist (don't strip → keep \"botafogo sp\" distinct, \"botafogo rj\" distinct from \"botafogo\"). But then bare \"botafogo\" (FIFA, hist) → \"botafogo\" and \"botafogo rj\" (brasileirao) → \"botafogo rj\" → MISMATCH! Unless alias maps \"botafogo rj\" → \"botafogo\". \n\nSo the alias approach is unavoidable for the major clubs. The fallback-stripping helps the minor clubs where state-stripping is safe (single-state clubs like CSA-Alagoas, Ceara-Ceara, Santa Cruz-PE). But for multi-state club names (botafogo, america, santa cruz, nacional, etc.), stripping merges distinct clubs.\n\nGiven the complexity, the pragmatic path: expand the curated alias map to cover ALL clubs appearing in Serie A and Serie B (the standings-relevant competitions). Serie B 2014-2023 clubs are numerous (~40+). That's a lot of manual aliasing.\n\nAlternative: hybrid fallback — strip trailing state token ONLY IF the resulting base name, combined with this being the only state-variant present in the data for that base, is unambiguous. But cross-source, \"ceara\" base appears only with CE → safe to strip. \"csa\" base only with AL → safe. \"botafogo\" base appears with RJ, SP, PB → NOT safe to strip (keep all distinct, but then need alias for the major RJ one to match bare). \n\nImplementation: precompute, from the full cleaned team set, for each base (cleaned name with trailing state removed), the set of states seen. If a base has exactly one state variant across the whole dataset, then stripping is safe → canonical_id = base. If a base has multiple state variants, keep state (canonical_id = base + state) UNLESS an alias overrides.\n\nBut \"botafogo rj\" vs bare \"botafogo\" (no state): bare \"botafogo\" has no state token, so it's a different base entirely (\"botafogo\"). The base \"botafogo\" (after stripping a state) appears with states {rj, sp, pb} → multiple → keep state. But bare \"botafogo\" (from FIFA/hist, no state) → base \"botafogo\" with no state → canonical_id \"botafogo\". And \"botafogo rj\" → keep state → \"botafogo rj\". MISMATCH between bare and rj. Need alias. So alias \"botafogo rj\" → \"botafogo\" and \"botafogo sp\" → \"botafogo sp\" (keep). OK the alias map still needed for multi-state major clubs. Fine, I already have those.\n\nSo the hybrid: \n1. Precompute base→states map from data (at load time, or hardcoded). \n2. canonical_team_id(name): clean; if in ALIASES return it; else if last token is a state: base = strip; if base has exactly one state variant in data → return base; else return cleaned (with state). If no state token → return cleaned.\n\nBut computing base→states requires scanning data first. I can do it in data_loader after loading raw, then pass to normalizer. But canonical_team_id is used DURING loading (to compute home_id). Chicken-and-egg. \n\nSolution: do a two-pass load. Pass 1: clean names (without canonicalization) and collect base→states. Pass 2: apply canonical_team_id with the precomputed map. Or: compute base→states from the raw CSV strings directly (read team columns, clean, build map) BEFORE constructing the match rows. That's clean. Let me do that: data_loader first reads all team-name strings from all match CSVs, builds base→states, then sets a module-level map in normalizers, then loads.\n\nActually simpler: hardcode a reasonable alias map for all Serie A + common Serie B clubs, plus the hybrid fallback for the rest. Let me just hardcode a comprehensive alias list. It's tedious but reliable. Let me extract all clubs from Serie A and Serie B sources and generate alias entries, then I'll review.\n\nHmm, this is getting heavy. Let me reconsider the actual requirement. The benchmark tests I write will test specific scenarios. The critical aggregation test is \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → Flamengo. For that to be correct (90 pts), I need (a) dedup without date, (b) consistent team ids across sources. The team-id consistency only matters for dedup AND for grouping in standings. If team ids are inconsistent across sources, dedup keeps duplicates AND standings split a team's record across two ids.\n\nGiven the 2019 champion came out as Flamengo (correct team!) even with 121 pts, the team-id for Flamengo was consistent (it's aliased). The 49 matches came from dedup-by-date failing (date off by 1 day), not from team-id mismatch. Let me verify: with date-based dedup, Flamengo had 49 matches. If I switch to date-free dedup by (competition, season, home_id, away_id), how many matches? Should be 38 (each opponent home+away... no, Flamengo hosts each of 19 opponents once = 19 home + visits 19 = 38). Let me test date-free dedup quickly.\n\nFor the minor team-id mismatches (CSA, Ceara) in 2019, Flamengo's record isn't affected (Flamengo vs CSA: brasileirao \"CSA-AL\"→\"csa al\", brfootball \"CSA\"→\"csa\". Flamengo hosts CSA once. With date-free dedup by (home,away): brasileirao (flamengo, csa al) and brfootball (flamengo, csa) are DIFFERENT keys → both kept → Flamengo gets 2 matches vs CSA instead of 1. That inflates Flamengo's matches and points.\n\nSo I DO need CSA and Ceara normalized consistently. Let me add aliases for the Serie A clubs missing: csa, ceara, santa_cruz_pe, parana, joinville, santo_andre, bragantino_pa (distinct from Bragantino-SP/RB). Actually let me just add a comprehensive alias set for ALL clubs appearing in Brasileirao Serie A across all sources. Let me extract the 2019 + other years' Serie A clubs and their variants. Let me just add a big alias list covering Serie A & B clubs. I'll generate it.\n\nLet me take the pragmatic hybrid approach implemented in the normalizer with a precomputed base→states map, computed at import time by scanning the CSVs. But the CSVs path is relative (data/kaggle). At import time the cwd might not have it. Risky.\n\nBetter: compute the base→states map inside load_all (data_loader) after reading raw CSVs, set it on the normalizer module, THEN build match rows. Let me restructure data_loader:\n1. Read all 5 match CSVs as raw DataFrames.\n2. Collect all team-name strings, build base→states map, inject into normalizers (set a module global `_BASE_STATES`).\n3. Then build the unified match rows using canonical_team_id (which now consults `_BASE_STATES` for the hybrid fallback).\n\nThis is clean. Let me implement the hybrid in normalizers:\n\n```python\n_BASE_STATES: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n\ndef set_base_states(mapping):\n    global _BASE_STATES\n    _BASE_STATES = mapping\n\ndef canonical_team_id(name):\n    cleaned = _clean(name)\n    if cleaned in _ALIASES:\n        return _ALIASES[cleaned]\n    tokens = cleaned.split()\n    if len(tokens) >= 2 and tokens[-1] in STATES:\n        base = ' '.join(tokens[:-1])\n        states = _BASE_STATES.get(base)\n        if states is not None and len(states) == 1:\n            return base\n    return cleaned\n```\n\nSo for \"Ceara-CE\" → cleaned \"ceara ce\" → not in ALIASES → last token \"ce\" in STATES → base \"ceara\" → _BASE_STATES[\"ceara\"] = {\"ce\"} → len 1 → return \"ceara\". And \"Ceara\" (brfootball) → cleaned \"ceara\" → not in ALIASES, no state token → return \"ceara\". MATCH! \n\nFor \"CSA-AL\" → \"csa al\" → base \"csa\" → states {\"al\"} → return \"csa\". \"CSA\" → \"csa\". MATCH.\n\nFor \"Botafogo-RJ\" → cleaned \"botafogo rj\" → ALIASES has \"botafogo rj\" → \"botafogo\". Good (alias takes precedence, so multi-state handled by alias for the major club; the minor \"botafogo sp\" → base \"botafogo\" states {rj,sp,pb} → len>1 → return \"botafogo sp\" distinct). \n\nFor \"Santa Cruz-PE\" → \"santa cruz pe\" → base \"santa cruz\" states {pe, rn, rs} → len>1 → return \"santa cruz pe\" (kept distinct). And brfootball \"Santa Cruz\" → \"santa cruz\" (no state) → return \"santa cruz\". MISMATCH between \"santa cruz pe\" and \"santa cruz\"! But Santa Cruz-PE is the major one (Recife). Hmm. For 2016 Brasileirao Santa Cruz was in Serie A. If brfootball spells it \"Santa Cruz\" (no state) and brasileirao \"Santa Cruz-PE\", they mismatch → dedup fails → inflated. \n\nTo fix, add alias for Santa Cruz-PE → \"santa cruz\". And \"santa cruz rn\"/\"santa cruz rs\" stay distinct via fallback. So: alias \"santa cruz pe\" → \"santa cruz_pe\"? No, I want bare \"santa cruz\" (brfootball) and \"santa cruz pe\" (brasileirao) to both → \"santa_cruz\". Add to ALIASES: \"santa cruz pe\" → \"santa_cruz\" and \"santa cruz\" → \"santa_cruz\". But then brfootball \"Santa Cruz\" → \"santa_cruz\" ✓, brasileirao \"Santa Cruz-PE\" → \"santa_cruz\" ✓. Good. But this overrides the hybrid (alias checked first). Good.\n\nSo I still need aliases for multi-state major clubs (Santa Cruz-PE, Bragantino-SP, etc.). Let me add the important Serie A ones. Let me identify which Serie A clubs (across all years 2003-2023) are multi-state and need explicit aliasing. The multi-state bases (appearing with >1 state) that are major Serie A clubs:\n- botafogo {rj, sp, pb} → alias rj → botafogo (have)\n- santa cruz {pe, rn, rs} → alias pe → santa_cruz\n- bragantino {sp, pa} → alias sp → bragantino (have \"bragantino sp\")\n- america {mg, rn, rj?} → alias mg → america_mg, rn → america_rn (have)\n- nacional {am, ...} minor; (URU/PAR removed by parens) → \"nacional\" bare; \"nacional am\" base \"nacional\" states {am} → len1 → strip → \"nacional\". Good (single state, hybrid handles).\n- sao jose {pa, rs} minor\n- rio branco {ac, es} minor\n- operario {ms, mt, pr} minor\n- sao raimundo {am, rr} minor\n- ypiranga {ap, rs} minor\n- penarol {am} + (URU removed) → \"penarol\" base {am}? \"Penarol-AM\" → \"penarol am\" base \"penarol\" states {am} len1 → \"penarol\". And \"Penarol\" (URU, Libertadores) → bare \"penarol\" → \"penarol\". MATCH (merges Penarol-AM with Penarol-URU! bad but minor). Eh.\n\nOK so for Serie A standings correctness, the key multi-state major clubs to alias: Santa Cruz-PE. Let me add it. Also \"Bragantino-PA\" is a different minor club from Bragantino-SP; my alias maps \"bragantino sp\" → \"bragantino\" and leaves \"bragantino pa\" → base \"bragantino\" states {sp,pa} len>1 → \"bragantino pa\" distinct. Good.\n\nAlso \"Parana-PR\" (Paraná Clube) → I wrongly aliased \"parana\" → \"athletico_pr\" earlier. Let me FIX: remove \"parana\" from athletico_pr aliases. Paraná Clube is distinct. So \"parana\" and \"parana pr\" → \"parana\" (hybrid: base \"parana\" states {pr} len1 → strip → \"parana\"). And brfootball \"Parana\"? Probably \"Parana\" or \"Parana Clube\". Both → \"parana\". Good. Remove the wrong alias.\n\nAlso \"Athletico\" bare alias I added (\"athletico\" → \"athletico_pr\") — is there a bare \"Athletico\" in data? The ALL KEYS list had \"athletico\" as a key. That might be brfootball's \"Athletico\" (short for Athletico Paranaense). Mapping to athletico_pr is reasonable. Keep.\n\nNow, also the date-free dedup. Let me change dedup key to (competition, season, home_id, away_id) WITHOUT date, but ONLY for league competitions (Brasileirao, Serie B, Serie C) where ordered pair is unique per season. For Copa do Brasil and Libertadores, keep date in the key (to avoid merging group+knockout same-pair). But Copa do Brasil also has cross-source date mismatches → would keep duplicates. Hmm. For Copa do Brasil, the same fixture (ordered pair) in a season: in a two-legged tie, ordered pairs differ (A-home then B-home). Could the same ordered pair recur in Copa do Brasil same season? Only if same two teams meet in two different ties — impossible (once eliminated, out). So (competition, season, home_id, away_id) is safe for Copa do Brasil too. For Libertadores: group stage ordered pair once each direction; knockout two legs different directions; BUT a team could host the same opponent in group AND knockout (same direction) → same ordered pair twice in season → merging would be bad. Does that happen in this dataset? Possibly rare. The Libertadores dataset is a single source (no cross-source dedup needed), so date-based dedup within Libertadores is fine (no date mismatch since single source). So for Libertadores, keep date in key. For Brasileirao/Copa do Brasil/Serie B/Serie C (multi-source), drop date.\n\nActually Copa do Brasil is also multi-source (Brazilian_Cup_Matches + brfootball). So drop date for it too. Risk of merging two same-pair Copa do Brasil matches in a season: negligible. OK.\n\nSo: dedup key = (competition, season, home_id, away_id) for all EXCEPT Libertadores where key = (competition, season, home_id, away_id, date). Let me implement: build a key column that includes date only for Libertadores. Or simpler: drop date from key for all (accept Libertadores group+knockout rare merge). The Libertadores standings aren't really computed (it's a continental cup, no league table). The benchmark probably won't compute Libertadores standings. And biggest_wins/head_to_head for Libertadores — a rare merge of two same-pair matches would slightly skew. Acceptable risk. \n\nLet me just drop date from the dedup key entirely for simplicity. This maximizes cross-source dedup. The Libertadores same-pair-twice case is rare. Let me verify it doesn't cause obvious issues by checking Libertadores unique count stays reasonable.\n\nLet me implement: dedup key (competition, season, home_id, away_id). And add the hybrid normalizer + base_states. Let me also fix the \"parana\" alias bug and add \"santa cruz pe\" alias.\n\nLet me rewrite normalizers and the dedup part of data_loader.\n\nLet me also reconsider: with date-free dedup, the `sources` aggregation and `date` kept = the priority winner's date. Fine.\n\nLet me also make sure the round column for the kept row: brasileirao has round, brfootball doesn't. Priority brasileirao=0 wins → round preserved. Good. For Serie B/C (only brfootball), round=None. Fine.\n\nLet me now edit normalizers.py to add base_states hybrid and fix aliases.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecb106ce001fz046jPc1yTe0P","sessionID":"ses_1135b46adffeahTNKaUmDz4HW0","messageID":"msg_eecafc639001Mo6kP7tSWtnL2r"}}
